  "self-esteem": {
    "higher": [
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "are able to act according to what they think to be the best choice, trusting their own judgment, and not feeling guilty when others do not like their choice"
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "fully trust their capacity to solve problems, not hesitating after failures and difficulties; they ask others for help when they need it"
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "are able to enjoy a great variety of activities"
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "consider themselves equal in dignity to others, rather than inferior or superior, while accepting differences in certain talents, personal prestige, or financial standing"
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "can work toward finding solutions and voice discontent without belittling themselves or others when challenges arise"
      }
    ],
    "average": [
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "Self-esteem tends to increase during adolescence and young adulthood, reaching a peak in middle age."
      }
    ],
    "lower": [
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "Individuals with low self-esteem tend to be critical of themselves."
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "However, this does not mean it cannot be changed."
      }
    ],
    "general": [
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "In terms of personality, emotionally stable, extroverted, and conscientious individuals experience higher self-esteem."
      }
    ],
    "development": [
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "Experiences in a person's life are a major source of how self-esteem develops."
      },
      {
        "source": "selfesteem",
        "text": "These predictors have shown that self-esteem has trait-like qualities by remaining stable over time like personality and intelligence."
      }
    ]
  },
  "attachment": {
    "secure": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "People that have a secure attachment style are often associated with high self-esteem, and have an easier time forming healthy relationships with others."
      },
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Those with a secure attachment style often manage conflict without feeling overwhelmed and are not bothered by the concepts of closeness and independence."
      }
    ],
    "anxious-preoccupied": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "These individuals seek intimacy but fear that others do not want to be with them."
      },
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Even in stable relationships, many anxious attachment individuals worry about the state of the relationship."
      }
    ],
    "dismissive-avoidant": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Individuals with a dismissive-avoidant attachment style typically suppress their emotions, and prioritize their personal independence."
      }
    ],
    "fearful-avoidant": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "They seek close relationships, but are scared of them, and so experience a personal battle within themselves."
      },
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Many people with this attachment style may stay in the dating stage of romantic relationships as it takes longer to trust others."
      }
    ],
    "general": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Around 70–80% of people experience no significant changes in attachment styles over time."
      },
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "suggest working models are not rigid personality traits"
      }
    ],
    "more": {
      "secure": [
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "Many adults who have a secure attachment style have an easier time trusting in themselves and their relationships."
        },
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "Adults who have a secure attachment style usually express greater satisfaction with their relationships."
        }
      ],
      "anxious-preoccupied": [
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "Individuals with anxious attachment seek approval by others regarding the security of the relationship."
        }
      ],
      "dismissive-avoidant": [
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "Many have a hard time opening up to romantic partners, and when things feel real, they pull away."
        },
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "The characteristics of individuals who are dismissive-avoidant include emotional distance, independence over closeness"
        }
      ],
      "fearful-avoidant": [
        {
          "source": "attachment",
          "text": "These individuals sometimes seek closeness, but other times crave distance due to fear of abandonment."
        }
      ]
    },
    "outcomes": [
      {
        "source": "attachment",
        "text": "Secure attachment styles may lead to more constructive communication and more intimate self-disclosures, which in turn increase relationship satisfaction."
      }
    ]
  },
  "rmet": {
    "general": [
      {
        "source": "tom",
        "text": "theory of mind (often abbreviated to ToM) is the capacity to understand other individuals by ascribing mental states to them."
      },
      {
        "source": "tom",
        "text": "Possessing a functional theory of mind is crucial for success in everyday human social interactions."
      },
      {
        "source": "tom",
        "text": "People use a theory of mind when analyzing, judging, and inferring other people's behaviors."
      }
    ],
    "caveat": [
      {
        "source": "tom",
        "text": "Measures such as the Reading the Mind in the Eyes test (RME) have been widely used and accepted by experts as measures of ToM but systematic reviews of research have shown a lack of validity data."
      }
    ]
  },
  "rorschach": {
    "about": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach_org",
        "text": "In the strictest sense, the Rorschach Inkblot Test is a test or assessment of perception."
      },
      {
        "source": "rorschach_org",
        "text": "How does someone view and organize the world around them?"
      },
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "\"Popularity\" and \"originality\" of responses can also be considered as basic dimensions in the analysis."
      },
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "different cultures will exhibit different \"common\" objects"
      }
    ],
    "caveat": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach_org_home",
        "text": "A self-evaluation online is by no means appropriate."
      }
    ],
    "research": [
      {
        "source": "dauphin2024",
        "text": "higher levels of Complexity are also associated with scanning more regions of the blot overall, albeit at a slower rate."
      }
    ],
    "I": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Being the first card, it can provide clues about how subjects tackle a new and stressful task."
      }
    ],
    "II": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "The red details of card II are often seen as blood, and are the most distinctive features."
      }
    ],
    "III": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Card III is typically perceived to contain two humans involved in some interaction, and may provide information about how the subject relates with other people"
      }
    ],
    "IV": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "is generally perceived as a big and sometimes threatening figure; compounded with the common impression of the subject being in an inferior position (\"looking up\") to it, this serves to elicit a sense of authority."
      }
    ],
    "V": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Card V is an easily elaborated card that is not usually perceived as threatening, and typically instigates a \"change of pace\" in the test, after the previous more challenging cards."
      }
    ],
    "VI": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Texture is the dominant characteristic of card VI, which often elicits association related to interpersonal closeness"
      }
    ],
    "VII": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Card VII can be associated with femininity (the human figures commonly seen in it being described as women or children)"
      }
    ],
    "VIII": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "People often express relief about card VIII, which lets them relax and respond effectively."
      }
    ],
    "IX": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Characteristic of card IX is indistinct form and diffuse, muted chromatic features, creating a general vagueness."
      }
    ],
    "X": [
      {
        "source": "rorschach",
        "text": "Being the last card, it may provide an opportunity for the subject to \"sign out\" by indicating what they feel their situation is like, or what they desire to know."
      }
    ]
  },
